  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 AM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nRidging persists over the western CONUS, extending from midlevel \nhigh pressure centered over the Four Corners. Meanwhile, midlevel \nlow pressure continues to spin over central Canada. We remain on the \nback side of an exiting shortwave, but increasingly this weekend, \nflow aloft becomes more zonal which leaves us vulnerable to passing \nshortwaves. In the meantime, behind the exiting cold front, skies \ncontinue to clear from west to east across the UP. Temperatures so \nfar have only fallen into the lower 60s in the western UP and the \nupper 60s under cloudy skies in the east. Expect temperatures to \nrange in the lower to mid 50s in the western UP, and the upper 50s \nto lower 60s in the east by the early morning hours. Surface ridging \ninto the Great Lakes today will allow for a sunny and pleasant day, \nwith temperatures coming in cooler than yesterday; expect highs in \nthe 70s for most, and closer to 80 in the south-central UP. NW winds \nmay turn breezy again, though not as much as on Friday, with gusts \nup to 15-20mph for the daytime hours. \n\n\nSunday brings back chances for showers and storms as another cold \nfront moves through. Modest instability and plenty of bulk shear \ncould support stronger storms, however better instability and \nforcing for severe storms remains south of the CWA. NBM \nprobabilities of exceeding 0.25 inches of rainfall are between 10-\n40%, highest over the west half of the UP. Otherwise quasi zonal \nflow over the Upper Great Lakes leads to pleasant summer temps in \nthe 70s the remainder of the forecast period with lows in the upper \n40s to 50s. Chances for showers and storms dot the extended forecast \nas transient shortwaves traverse the area.",
